Subject: A collection that follows one which found little garbage waits until its cost fits a share of the run time

What this does
  - quickjs.c js_gc_passes: after every collection records what it found against what it swept (a young one swept the allocation since the previous collection, a full one the heap): gc_yield_ok is set when at least a sixteenth of that was garbage; it also accumulates the collector's cycles (gc_spent) and the heap size it left (gc_after_last).
  - quickjs.c js_gc_admit: asked by the allocation-threshold path and by the safepoint collection in js_drc_reconcile. It admits the request when the last collection was productive (and before any has run). Otherwise it admits it only if gc_spent plus an estimate of the sweep (3 cycles per byte walked) fits in a sixteenth of the time since the runtime started plus 20M cycles, or if the heap has reached eight times what the last collection left and 192 MB, or half of a set memory limit. A refused request moves the threshold up to the heap size plus half and returns. QJS_GC_NOADMIT=1 admits everything; QJS_GC_STATS prints the number refused.

Why we need it
  splay builds a 128 MB live tree. With 0511-0517 the interpreter ran two young collections (454K objects examined, none freed, 200M cycles) and two early full ones; against the build without them splay lost 12% of its cycles (user cycles 2.63G against 2.32G; the integrator's score table 7260-7501 against 8827-8989). The pacing doubled the interval after an unproductive pass but the passes still swept ever larger mostly-live windows. With the admission rule splay runs one cheap full collection and the teardown one: 2.29-2.32G cycles, the level of the build with only 0510. deltablue (97% of every young window is garbage) and the other cyclic-garbage programs are never refused. typescript, whose young passes find 9%, is refused more often (5.99 s against 8.66 s on a busy machine, peak RSS 232 MB against 140 MB).

Soundness
  Only the decision to collect changes. Deferring a collection leaves cyclic garbage alive longer, which is bounded by the three limits above; reference counting still frees everything else. Nothing else in the collectors changes.

Failure modes
  A program that builds a large structure for a long time and then starts making cyclic garbage collects late: the first collection waits for the 8x/192 MB rule or the time budget; memory use is higher in the meantime (typescript +65% peak RSS). The thresholds (a sixteenth for the yield, a sixteenth for the time share, 20M cycles, 3 cycles per byte, 192 MB) are policy constants in js_gc_admit.

